;;; `std.closure/infer-closure` — closed and **owing**: a state that can never
;;; meet its requirement, reported as a model.
;;;
;;; `02_closed_and_satisfied.ein` with one fact deleted — the witness edge
;;; `(r a1 b1)` — and **the verdict does not move**. Both report `Solution`.
;;; That is the finding this file exists to hold still.
;;;
;;; ── Why nothing catches it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `a1` is an `A`, `r` is declared total, and `r` has no edges. So the
;;; requirement *every A has some B* is unmet. It is also **unreachable**:
;;;
;;;   - no rule derives an `r`-fact — there is no rule here but the observer;
;;;   - no hypothesis can propose one, because `functional ∧ total` derived
;;;     `(__closed__ r)` and closure is exactly the instruction never to
;;;     speculate about `r`.
;;;
;;; And `std.algebra/total` — the scan whose job is to say *the requirement
;;; has become unreachable* — cannot fire, because it is written
;;; open-world-safe: it demands a **stored negative** for every candidate
;;; (`(forall ?b (?isa ?b ?B) (not (?R ?a ?b)))`) before it concludes
;;; anything, and in a program with no negative-completion rule no such
;;; negative will ever be stored. The scan is activated, correct, and silent.
;;;
;;; Nothing is left to check the requirement, so the engine falls back on the
;;; only completeness test it has — *does the generator propose anything?* —
;;; and the generator, having been closed, proposes nothing. Complete by
;;; exhaustion; owing, by discharge.
;;;
;;; ── The three ingredients, and each is individually reasonable ─────────────
;;;
;;; Worth stating because no one of them is the bug:
;;;
;;;   1. `infer-closure`'s operational witness is opt-in and boxed-warned
;;;      (`01_infer_closure.ein` exhibits its completeness hazard);
;;;   2. `total`'s stored-negative discipline is what stops it firing `(false)`
;;;      on every empty-yet state, and is the *right* way to write it —
;;;      `connex` is what the other way looks like;
;;;   3. `complete(kb)` meaning "the generator proposes nothing" is what the
;;;      engine has, because it has no vocabulary for *what a state owes*.
;;;
;;; The corner is their intersection, and it is the closed-and-owing case
;;; [S1d.2.2](../../../plans/m1d_satisfiability/p1d.2_obligations/s1d.2.2_domains.md)
;;; asks who is allowed to say `(false)` about. The answer this program pins
;;; is *nobody, today*. It is banked as a golden precisely so that the stage
;;; that changes it has to move this file and say why.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation observes ──────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The same lift as the twin: `closed-here` makes the derived closure
;;; nameable. The expectation says the closure was derived — it does **not**
;;; say the state is a model, because `:expect` has no word for a state that
;;; owes something, which is the other half of the same gap.

(import std.algebra :symbols (functional total))
(import std.closure :symbols (infer-closure))

(relation is-a        T T :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation r           A B :why "{?1} is assigned {?2}")
(relation closed-here T   :why "{?1}'s extension was closed by infer-closure")

(is-a a1 A)
(is-a b1 B) (is-a b2 B)

;; The markers `infer-closure` reads (arity 1) …
(functional r)
(total      r)
;; … and the activator the unreachability scan needs (arity 2). It is
;; activated here and it never fires — see above.
(total r is-a)

(rule closed-observed ()
  :match  (__closed__ ?R)
  :assert (closed-here ?R)
  :priority 500
  :why    "{?R}'s extension is closed")

;; No `(r a1 b1)`. `a1` owes a `B` it can never be given.

(query
  :goal (closed-here ?R)
  ;; `r` is closed by derivation; the other two are scoped out by hand so the
  ;; blind generator is quiet and the verdict is about the requirement alone.
  :no-hypothesis (is-a closed-here)
  :expect (model (closed-here r)))
